wdraft - drag-and-drop source/sink for Wayland, without a window

Many programs, particularly web applications, only accept files that are
dragged into them. The usual answer is a helper that opens a window full of
draggable buttons -- but that means a visible window, a toolkit dependency,
and actually dragging with the mouse from the window to the target.

wdraft does the same job with no window and no dragging. Files go into a
stash, you put the pointer over the drop target, and a keybind does the drop:

  wdraft add photo.png          # stash it
  <hover the upload area>
  <press your keybind>          # bindsym $mod+d exec wdraft drop

The pointer never moves and nothing is ever drawn on screen. The stash
survives across drops until you clear it, so you can stash once and drop into
several places.

Other direction: wdraft catch turns the whole screen into an invisible drop
target and prints whatever is dropped on it to standard output.

For targets that take a paste but not a drop, wdraft copy puts the stash on
the clipboard as text/uri-list.

Try it without setting up a keybind by giving yourself time to aim:

  wdraft add photo.png && sleep 3 && wdraft -v drop

See wdraft(1) for all commands, options and exit codes.

How it works
------------
Wayland deliberately does not let a client begin a drag out of nowhere:
wl_data_device.start_drag requires a serial from a real button event on a
surface the client itself owns. The clipboard protocols are no help either --
ext-data-control and wlr-data-control cover selections, not drag-and-drop.

So wdraft manufactures that event. It maps a fully transparent fullscreen
layer-shell surface, which captures the pointer exactly where it already is,
and synthesises a left-button press on it through wlr-virtual-pointer. With
the serial in hand it starts the drag, then makes its own surface
input-transparent so the compositor hands the drag session to the real target
underneath, waits for that target to accept, and releases the button. The
compositor performs the drop.

The "invisible space" holding your files needs no running process: the stash
is a plain file in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wdraft/stash. There is no daemon.

Requirements
------------
A compositor with wlr-layer-shell and wlr-virtual-pointer -- in practice a
wlroots-based one: sway, Hyprland, river, Wayfire, labwc, niri. GNOME and KDE
do not expose wlr-virtual-pointer, so drop cannot work there; catch and copy
need only layer-shell and ext-data-control respectively.

wdraft is a Wayland program; there is no X11 backend.

Building
--------
Build dependencies: wayland-client, wayland-scanner, wayland-protocols.
No GTK, no runtime dependencies beyond libwayland-client.

  make

Two wlroots protocol descriptions are vendored in protocols/ because no
distribution packages them reliably; everything else comes from
wayland-protocols via pkg-config.

To install, run `make install`, which puts it into ~/.local by default. Use
`make PREFIX=/path/here install` for somewhere else, or
`make NAME=other-name install` to rename the binary, man page and completion.

wdraft is distributed under the GNU GPL version 3.
